<Legal
  title="Privacy Policy"
  updated="17 August 2026"
  description="How datadir handles data for poddle — and why the product is built to hold as little of yours as possible."
>
  <p class="lead">
    poddle is built to hold as little of your data as possible. The broker keeps credentials in a
    sealed vault; your code and prompts run in pods on infrastructure you control. This policy explains
    what we do collect, and why.
  </p>

  <p>
    This Privacy Policy describes how <strong>datadir s. r. o.</strong> ("datadir", "we", "us"),
    operator of poddle, processes personal data. It applies to poddle.dev and the poddle service.
    We are the controller for that data. Registered office: <strong>[registered address]</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2>What we collect</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Account data</strong> — your name, email, and organisation, so you can sign in and we can contact you.</li>
    <li><strong>Billing data</strong> — handled by our payment processor; we store a customer reference and invoices, not full card details.</li>
    <li><strong>Usage &amp; telemetry</strong> — coarse product events and error diagnostics to keep the service reliable and secure.</li>
    <li><strong>Audit records</strong> — which identity performed which action and when, so your team has an accountable trail.</li>
    <li><strong>Support communications</strong> — anything you send us when you ask for help.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>What we do <em>not</em> want</h3>
  <p>
    We do not want your vendor secrets or your source code. Credentials are sealed in the broker vault
    and injected on the wire; pods run on your own cloud or self-hosted compute. We are not in the path
    of your repositories or your prompts, and we do not train models on your data.
  </p>

  <h2>How we use it</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>To provide, secure, and improve the service.</li>
    <li>To bill you and prevent fraud.</li>
    <li>To respond to support requests and send essential service notices.</li>
    <li>To meet legal and security obligations.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Legal bases (GDPR)</h2>
  <p>
    We process personal data to perform our contract with you, on the basis of our legitimate interests
    in running a secure and reliable service, to comply with legal obligations, and — where required —
    with your consent, which you may withdraw at any time.
  </p>

  <h2>Sharing &amp; processors</h2>
  <p>
    We share data only with vetted sub-processors that help us run poddle — cloud hosting, payment
    processing, error monitoring, and email delivery — each under a data-processing agreement. We do
    not sell personal data. A current list of sub-processors is available on request.
  </p>

  <h2>Retention</h2>
  <p>
    We keep account and audit data for the life of your account and as long as needed for legal,
    accounting, or security purposes, then delete or anonymise it. You can ask us to delete your
    account data at any time.
  </p>

  <h2>Your rights</h2>
  <p>
    Under the GDPR you may request access, rectification, erasure, restriction, portability, and object
    to certain processing. To exercise any of these, email
    <a href="mailto:privacy@datadir.co">privacy@datadir.co</a>. You also have the right to lodge a
    complaint with your local supervisory authority.
  </p>

  <h2>International transfers</h2>
  <p>
    Where data is transferred outside the EEA, we rely on appropriate safeguards such as the European
    Commission's Standard Contractual Clauses.
  </p>

  <h2>Cookies</h2>
  <p>
    poddle.dev uses only what's necessary to serve the site and, where enabled, privacy-friendly,
    cookieless analytics. We do not use advertising trackers.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>We'll post any changes here and update the date above; material changes will be notified to account holders.</p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>Questions or requests: <a href="mailto:privacy@datadir.co">privacy@datadir.co</a>.</p>

  <p class="note">
    This is a working draft intended as a starting point and should be reviewed by counsel before
    launch. Bracketed items and processor lists need to be completed with datadir's specifics.
  </p>
</Legal>
